the top two purposes of every for-profit  company is to offer as much value as  possible and bring home as much profit  as possible  some companies are particularly good at  providing value while others are  phenomenal at generating profits but  very few companies are able to strike a  balance that pleases everyone one  company that has managed to achieve this  however is visa  generally it's the retailer or seller  paying credit card and debit card fees  so most customers never bat an eye to  them using a card is much more  convenient than carrying cash and change  and as for sellers most of them view the  one to three percent processing fee as  an acceptable investment because  accepting cards brings in so much more  traffic i mean just imagine where online  sales would be if it wasn't for cards on  the other side of the coin visa is  pretty happy as well even though they  only make a small fee on each  transaction americans alone spent 6.7  trillion through credit and debit cards  in 2019 and 60 of that was through visa  aside from pulling in massive revenue  visa has zero cogs or cost of goods sold  which means that their gross profit  margin is literally 100  even after you account for operating  expenses and taxes visa is able to bring  in 51 of their revenue as net profit  so here's the story of the most  profitable company in the world  taking a look back the origin of visa  dates all the way back to 1956 to bank  of america  at the time credit cards were already  quite popular amongst middle class  americans but the system was by no means  centralized or streamlined generally  merchants issued their own cards that  could be used to make purchases at their  stores and be paid off at the end of the  month but the problem was that each card  was specific to the merchant so they had  to carry a card for every retailer they  shopped at and at the end of the month  they had to pay off several bills  ironically the situation is basically  the same today as people use different  cards for groceries travel and gas to  maximize on their points but  theoretically people can just get away  with one or two credit cards if they  wanted to in the 1950s however this was  not possible many smaller banks had  already tried to create an all-around  credit card but given their relatively  small customer bases these never picked  up steam so when joseph p williams the  product development leader at bank of  america suggested creating a credit card  executives weren't quite convinced they  feared that not enough americans would  adopt the system and that merchants  would fight back but after a bit of  convincing it joseph was able to get  approval for a soft launch in fresno  california the executives felt that  fresno was big enough to give the system  a shot but it was also small enough that  if things went bad the company wouldn't  lose too much money something else to  note is that bank of america had 45  market share in fresno so you could say  that they had the cards stacked in their  favor  anyway on september 18th 1958 bank of  america finally launched bank americart  by shipping 65 000 cards to fresno  residents  the launch in fresno actually went  pretty well and bank of america was  feeling comfortable in expanding their  program but then rumors started to roll  around that another major bank was going  to launch their own credit card in san  francisco san francisco was bank of  america's home market so they couldn't  just let some competitor beat them to  market bank of america doubled down on  bank americart and pumped up card  issuance through the roof by march of  1959 they had sent out cards in san  francisco and sacramento by june they  added los angeles and by october they  had issued over 2 million credit cards  in terms of popularity bank americart  was a massive success as 20 000  merchants were accepting the card in  terms of media and profitability though  bank americart was having a rough time  you see joseph had vastly underestimated  the number of people who would take  advantage of the service joseph  anticipated that only four percent of  accounts would become delinquent but the  actual number came in at 22 percent in  the meantime bank of mere cards were the  perfect tool for fraudsters leading to  the rise of credit card fraud now bank  of america had thought about this and  their terms of service specified that  customers would be liable for fraudulent  charges  but the media and politicians just ate  this up and destroyed the reputation of  bank americart by 1960 bank of america  had racked up 20 million losses and the  joseph would throw in the towel by  resigning bank of america however was  determined to make this work they had  seen how quickly merchants and customers  adopted the system and they knew that  they had something massive on their  hands bank of america simply sent out an  apology to their 3 million customers and  they simply stayed the course  by may of 1961 the venture would turn  profitable but bank of america didn't  make a fuss about it they let the media  continue to paint bank americart as a  tool for fosters as this simply deterred  competitors from entering the space  this strategy worked for a couple of  years but by 1966 it was clear that bank  americard was making bank  initially bank americart was only meant  for california because federal laws  restricted bank of america from directly  expanding to other states but an  up-and-coming competitor called master  charge had figured out a workaround  mastercharge had created an alliance  between a bunch of regional banks to  circumvent federal laws seeing this bank  of america decided to do the same thing  and given their brand power this  strategy was extremely powerful starting  in 1966 bank of america began signing  licensing agreements with banks outside  of the state but they soon came across  another federal hurdle  in 1970 the federal government would  outlaw banks from sending unsolicited  credit cards to americans so bank of  america could no longer just send their  cards to as many people as they could  and hope that people would bite but  really this was a bigger problem for  competitors than bank of america bank of  america had already sent out over 100  million credit cards by the time this  law came around so the law really just  stifled competition in the meantime bank  of america took this opportunity to  focus on international expansion between  1968 and 1977 for example bank of  america partnered with an alliance of  canadian banks to issue a credit card  named chargex  similarly they would bring out carte  blue and friends and they also brought  out options in japan the uk and spain  but as the card spread around the world  bank america started to become a larger  and larger part of bank of america bank  americart was no longer bank of  america's card it was simply a popular  card that was owned by bank of america  in other words bank americart was  becoming an independent business that  was no longer reliant on bank of america  and the bank of america was actually  stifling the reach of bank americart  here's the thing not everyone in the  world is big fans of america especially  during the cold war and the inflation  crisis of the 1970s many international  customers refused to get a card that was  associated with bank of america if the  bank was named chase or wells fargo they  might have had an easier time with  international customers but their name  was literally bank of america now bank  of america isn't actually a central or  national bank like the bank of canada or  the swiss national bank but the name  does create such an image aside from  this association bank americart's  dealings became increasingly messy as  bank of america signed deals with  various banks around the world they  agreed to varying transaction fees  bearing licensing fees and varying terms  and conditions this made the system  extremely unorganized and difficult to  manage so in 1970 de hawk the chairman  of the bank of marquardt committee  proposed the idea of spinning off bank  americart into its own company bank of  america would agree and a new company  called nbi or national bank america dink  was created for domestic relations and  the d-hawk would be made ceo however  bank of america retained a right to  continue issuing bank america arts  outside of the u.s and forming new  partnerships bank of america wasn't  actually that good at this though and it  didn't take long for this to turn into a  massive mess as well by 1972  international partners were starting to  get frustrated with bank of america and  wanted a more streamlined process so  bank of america would seek help from  d-hawk eventually they would work out a  deal and create a new company called  ebenko or international bank card  company for international relations  this new system of ebenko and nbi was  actually pretty effective but by 1976  the companies came to the conclusion  that there was no reason to operate as  separate entities so in 1976 the two  companies merged and created what we now  know as visa  now that all of the background issues  were taken care of visa could focus on  improving the service this included  supporting more currencies offering cash  withdrawals around the clock launching  the debit card and so on and so forth  but by far their biggest challenge was  addressing credit card fraud  unfortunately this couldn't really be  addressed until technology advanced much  more but visa introduced fraud  protection services as soon as they  could  in 1993 for instance visa started  utilizing neural network technology to  better identify fraudulent payments but  their biggest step against fraud didn't  come till 2004 when they introduced  advanced authorization  advanced authorization leverages machine  learning and risk management tools to  assign a risk core to every visa  transaction in real time  for example if you go grocery shopping  every friday and one week you go on  wednesday that payment would be assigned  a higher risk score similarly if you  live in austin but go on vacation to new  york and make a purchase there that  transaction would have a much higher  risk score while this system is quite  effective and has allowed for more  consumer friendly fraud policies critics  have argued that the system is intrusive  in order to identify these anomalies  visa needed to know your purchasing  habits and travel life pretty well  despite these concerns though visa's  growth has never slowed down as consumer  spending just keeps going up year after  year in 1997 visa reached 1 trillion in  annual payment volume within just 10  years though this over quadrupled to 4.4  trillion dollars in 2008 visa would go  public and since then the stock has run  over 1100  making them the 13th largest company in  the world with a valuation of 438  billion dollars and when you control  their credit card market and have 500  margins that's really not that  surprising do you guys have a visa  powered credit card comment that down  below  also drop a like if you're a master at  milking credit card rewards and of  course consider joining our discord  community to suggest futurity ideas and  consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys on  the next one  [Music]  you

